2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:03,280
Today we are continuing our

3
00:00:03,280 --> 00:00:05,240
implementation of makemore.

4
00:00:05,240 --> 00:00:06,920
Now in the last lecture we implemented

5
00:00:06,920 --> 00:00:08,520
the bigram language model and we

6
00:00:08,520 --> 00:00:11,200
implemented both using counts and also

7
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:13,080
using a super simple neural network that

8
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:15,560
had a single linear layer.

9
00:00:15,560 --> 00:00:17,600
Now this is the

10
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:19,320
Jupyter notebook that we built out last

11
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:20,600
lecture.

12
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:21,720
And we saw that the way we approached

13
00:00:21,720 --> 00:00:23,360
this is that we looked at only the

14
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:25,000
single previous character and we

15
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:26,480
predicted the distribution for the

16
00:00:26,480 --> 00:00:28,280
character that would go next in the

17
00:00:28,280 --> 00:00:30,360
sequence. And we did that by taking

18
00:00:30,360 --> 00:00:32,320
counts and normalizing them into

19
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:33,760
probabilities

20
00:00:33,760 --> 00:00:36,840
so that each row here sums to one.

21
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:38,600
Now this is all well and good if you

22
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:40,480
only have one character of previous

23
00:00:40,480 --> 00:00:41,680
context.

24
00:00:41,680 --> 00:00:43,880
And this works and it's approachable.

25
00:00:43,880 --> 00:00:45,360
The problem with this model of course is

26
00:00:45,360 --> 00:00:48,120
that the predictions from this model are

27
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:50,160
not very good because you only take one

28
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:52,600
character of context. So the model

29
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:54,440
didn't produce very name-like sounding

30
00:00:54,440 --> 00:00:56,200
things.

31
00:00:56,200 --> 00:00:57,920
Now the problem with this approach

32
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:00,000
though is that if we are to take more

33
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,960
context into account when predicting the

34
00:01:01,960 --> 00:01:03,760
next character in the sequence, things

35
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:06,120
quickly blow up and this table the size

36
00:01:06,120 --> 00:01:08,400
of this table grows and in fact it grows

37
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:10,000
exponentially with the length of the

38
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:11,320
context.

39
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:12,360
Because if we only take a single

40
00:01:12,360 --> 00:01:13,920
character at a time, that's 27

41
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:16,080
possibilities of context.

42
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:17,840
But if we take two characters in the

43
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:19,760
past and try to predict the third one,

44
00:01:19,760 --> 00:01:21,680
suddenly the number of rows in this

45
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:23,880
matrix, you can look at it that way,

46
00:01:23,880 --> 00:01:27,360
is 27 * 27. So there's 729 possibilities

47
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:30,280
for what could have come in the context.

48
00:01:30,280 --> 00:01:31,800
If we take three characters as the

49
00:01:31,800 --> 00:01:34,440
context, suddenly we have

50
00:01:34,440 --> 00:01:37,680
20,000 possibilities of context.

51
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:40,120
And so that's just way too many rows of

52
00:01:40,120 --> 00:01:43,680
this matrix. It's way too few counts

53
00:01:43,680 --> 00:01:45,560
for each possibility and the whole thing

54
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:47,400
just kind of explodes and doesn't work

55
00:01:47,400 --> 00:01:49,120
very well.

56
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:50,480
So that's why today we're going to move

57
00:01:50,480 --> 00:01:52,400
on to this bullet point here and we're

58
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:53,800
going to implement a multi-layer

59
00:01:53,800 --> 00:01:57,120
perceptron model to predict the next uh

60
00:01:57,120 --> 00:01:59,640
in a sequence. And this modeling

61
00:01:59,640 --> 00:02:00,760
approach that we're going to adopt

62
00:02:00,760 --> 00:02:04,360
follows this paper Bengio et al. 2003.

63
00:02:04,360 --> 00:02:06,400
So, I have the paper pulled up here.

64
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:07,960
Now, this isn't the very first paper

65
00:02:07,960 --> 00:02:10,080
that proposed the use of uh multi-layer

66
00:02:10,080 --> 00:02:11,680
perceptrons or neural networks to

67
00:02:11,680 --> 00:02:13,720
predict the next character or token in a

68
00:02:13,720 --> 00:02:15,560
sequence, but it's definitely one that

69
00:02:15,560 --> 00:02:17,560
is uh was very influential around that

70
00:02:17,560 --> 00:02:19,880
time. It is very often cited to stand in

71
00:02:19,880 --> 00:02:21,680
for this idea, and I think it's a very

72
00:02:21,680 --> 00:02:23,680
nice write-up. And so, this is the paper

73
00:02:23,680 --> 00:02:25,520
that we're going to first look at and

74
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:27,840
then implement. Now, this paper has 19

75
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:30,280
pages. So, we don't have time to go into

76
00:02:30,280 --> 00:02:31,760
the full detail of this paper, but I

77
00:02:31,760 --> 00:02:33,640
invite you to read it. Uh it's very

78
00:02:33,640 --> 00:02:34,959
readable, interesting, and has a lot of

79
00:02:34,959 --> 00:02:37,120
interesting ideas in it as well.

80
00:02:37,120 --> 00:02:38,400
In the introduction, they describe the

81
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:40,720
exact same problem I just described. And

82
00:02:40,720 --> 00:02:42,360
then to address it, they propose the

83
00:02:42,360 --> 00:02:43,959
following model.

84
00:02:43,959 --> 00:02:46,600
Now, keep in mind that we are building a

85
00:02:46,600 --> 00:02:48,080
character-level language model. So,

86
00:02:48,080 --> 00:02:49,000
we're working on the level of

87
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,280
characters. In this paper, they have a

88
00:02:51,280 --> 00:02:54,280
vocabulary of 17,000 possible words, and

89
00:02:54,280 --> 00:02:56,200
they instead built a word-level language

90
00:02:56,200 --> 00:02:57,920
model. But, we're going to still stick

91
00:02:57,920 --> 00:02:59,239
with the characters, but we'll take the

92
00:02:59,239 --> 00:03:01,280
same modeling approach.

93
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:03,519
Now, what they do is basically they

94
00:03:03,519 --> 00:03:04,800
propose to take every one of these

95
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:07,280
words, 17,000 words, and they're going

96
00:03:07,280 --> 00:03:10,040
to associate to each word a say

97
00:03:10,040 --> 00:03:12,720
30-dimensional feature vector.

98
00:03:12,720 --> 00:03:16,440
So, every word is now embedded into a

99
00:03:16,440 --> 00:03:18,200
30-dimensional space. You can think of

100
00:03:18,200 --> 00:03:21,320
it that way. So, we have 17,000 points

101
00:03:21,320 --> 00:03:23,880
or vectors in a 30-dimensional space,

102
00:03:23,880 --> 00:03:25,800
and that's um you might imagine that's

103
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:27,280
very crowded. That's a lot of points for

104
00:03:27,280 --> 00:03:28,880
a very small space.

105
00:03:28,880 --> 00:03:30,120
Now,

106
00:03:30,120 --> 00:03:31,280
in the beginning, these words are

107
00:03:31,280 --> 00:03:32,760
initialized completely randomly. So,

108
00:03:32,760 --> 00:03:34,760
they're spread out at random.

109
00:03:34,760 --> 00:03:36,560
But, then we're going to tune these

110
00:03:36,560 --> 00:03:38,640
embeddings of these words using back

111
00:03:38,640 --> 00:03:40,640
propagation. So, during the course of

112
00:03:40,640 --> 00:03:42,280
training of this neural network, these

113
00:03:42,280 --> 00:03:43,920
points or vectors are going to basically

114
00:03:43,920 --> 00:03:46,280
move around in this space. And you might

115
00:03:46,280 --> 00:03:48,160
imagine that, for example, words that

116
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:50,120
have very similar meanings or that are

117
00:03:50,120 --> 00:03:52,040
indeed synonyms of each other might end

118
00:03:52,040 --> 00:03:54,200
up in a very similar part of the space.

119
00:03:54,200 --> 00:03:55,680
And conversely, words that mean very

120
00:03:55,680 --> 00:03:57,480
different things would go somewhere else

121
00:03:57,480 --> 00:03:59,360
in the space.

122
00:03:59,360 --> 00:04:01,040
Now, their modeling approach otherwise

123
00:04:01,040 --> 00:04:03,400
is identical to ours. They are using a

124
00:04:03,400 --> 00:04:04,840
multi-layer neural network to predict

125
00:04:04,840 --> 00:04:07,400
the next word given the previous words.

126
00:04:07,400 --> 00:04:08,920
And to train the neural network, they

127
00:04:08,920 --> 00:04:10,720
are maximizing the log likelihood of the

128
00:04:10,720 --> 00:04:12,959
training data, just like we did.

129
00:04:12,959 --> 00:04:14,760
So, the modeling approach itself is

130
00:04:14,760 --> 00:04:16,359
identical. Now, here they have a

131
00:04:16,359 --> 00:04:18,959
concrete example of this intuition.

132
00:04:18,959 --> 00:04:20,359
Why does it work?

133
00:04:20,359 --> 00:04:21,840
Basically, suppose that for example, you

134
00:04:21,840 --> 00:04:23,760
are trying to predict "A dog was running

135
00:04:23,760 --> 00:04:25,760
in a blank."

136
00:04:25,760 --> 00:04:27,800
Now, suppose that the exact phrase "A

137
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:30,480
dog was running in a" has never occurred

138
00:04:30,480 --> 00:04:31,880
in the training data.

139
00:04:31,880 --> 00:04:33,960
And here you are at uh sort of test time

140
00:04:33,960 --> 00:04:35,400
later when the model is deployed

141
00:04:35,400 --> 00:04:36,440
somewhere.

142
00:04:36,440 --> 00:04:38,600
And it's trying to make a sentence, and

143
00:04:38,600 --> 00:04:40,440
it's saying "A dog was running in a

144
00:04:40,440 --> 00:04:41,480
blank."

145
00:04:41,480 --> 00:04:43,040
And because it's never encountered this

146
00:04:43,040 --> 00:04:45,480
exact phrase in the training set, you're

147
00:04:45,480 --> 00:04:47,600
out of distribution, as we say. Like,

148
00:04:47,600 --> 00:04:49,680
you don't have fundamentally any

149
00:04:49,680 --> 00:04:52,160
reason to suspect um

150
00:04:52,160 --> 00:04:54,120
what might come next.

151
00:04:54,120 --> 00:04:55,720
But, this approach actually allows you

152
00:04:55,720 --> 00:04:57,320
to get around that. Because maybe you

153
00:04:57,320 --> 00:04:59,080
didn't see the exact phrase "A dog was

154
00:04:59,080 --> 00:05:01,000
running in a something." But, maybe

155
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:02,640
you've seen similar phrases. Maybe

156
00:05:02,640 --> 00:05:04,240
you've seen the phrase "The dog was

157
00:05:04,240 --> 00:05:06,320
running in a blank."

158
00:05:06,320 --> 00:05:07,720
And maybe your network has learned that

159
00:05:07,720 --> 00:05:10,400
A and the are like frequently are

160
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:12,080
interchangeable with each other. And so,

161
00:05:12,080 --> 00:05:14,480
maybe it took the embedding for A and

162
00:05:14,480 --> 00:05:16,120
the embedding for the, and it actually

163
00:05:16,120 --> 00:05:17,560
put them like nearby each other in the

164
00:05:17,560 --> 00:05:19,320
space. And so, you can transfer

165
00:05:19,320 --> 00:05:21,480
knowledge through that embedding, and

166
00:05:21,480 --> 00:05:23,400
you can generalize in that way.

167
00:05:23,400 --> 00:05:25,040
Similarly, the network could know that

168
00:05:25,040 --> 00:05:26,840
cats and dogs are animals, and they

169
00:05:26,840 --> 00:05:28,600
co-occur in lots of very similar

170
00:05:28,600 --> 00:05:30,560
contexts. And so, even though you

171
00:05:30,560 --> 00:05:32,600
haven't seen this exact phrase,

172
00:05:32,600 --> 00:05:34,240
or if you haven't seen exactly walking

173
00:05:34,240 --> 00:05:36,520
or running, you can through the

174
00:05:36,520 --> 00:05:38,919
embedding space transfer knowledge, and

175
00:05:38,919 --> 00:05:42,280
you can generalize to novel scenarios.

176
00:05:42,280 --> 00:05:43,640
So, let's now scroll down to the diagram

177
00:05:43,640 --> 00:05:45,240
of the neural network. Uh they have a

178
00:05:45,240 --> 00:05:47,480
nice uh diagram here.

179
00:05:47,480 --> 00:05:49,960
And in this example, we are taking three

180
00:05:49,960 --> 00:05:51,920
previous words

181
00:05:51,920 --> 00:05:53,200
and we are trying to predict the fourth

182
00:05:53,200 --> 00:05:54,200
word

183
00:05:54,200 --> 00:05:56,040
in the sequence.

184
00:05:56,040 --> 00:05:57,720
Now, these three previous words, as I

185
00:05:57,720 --> 00:05:59,840
mentioned, we have a vocabulary of

186
00:05:59,840 --> 00:06:01,560
17,000

187
00:06:01,560 --> 00:06:03,000
possible words.

188
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:04,880
So, every one of these

189
00:06:04,880 --> 00:06:08,200
basically are the index of the incoming

190
00:06:08,200 --> 00:06:09,400
word.

191
00:06:09,400 --> 00:06:11,800
And because there are 17,000 words, this

192
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:16,880
is an integer between 0 and 16,999.

193
00:06:17,320 --> 00:06:19,440
Now, there's also a lookup table that

194
00:06:19,440 --> 00:06:21,040
they call C.

195
00:06:21,040 --> 00:06:22,840
This lookup table is a matrix that is

196
00:06:22,840 --> 00:06:26,040
17,000 by, say, 30.

197
00:06:26,040 --> 00:06:27,360
And basically what we're doing here is

198
00:06:27,360 --> 00:06:29,720
we're treating this as a lookup table.

199
00:06:29,720 --> 00:06:32,720
And so, every index is plucking out a

200
00:06:32,720 --> 00:06:35,360
row of this embedding matrix

201
00:06:35,360 --> 00:06:37,440
so that each index is converted to the

202
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:39,840
30-dimensional vector that corresponds

203
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:42,960
to the embedding vector for that word.

204
00:06:42,960 --> 00:06:45,680
So, here we have the input layer of 30

205
00:06:45,680 --> 00:06:48,840
neurons for three words, making up 90

206
00:06:48,840 --> 00:06:50,800
neurons in total.

207
00:06:50,800 --> 00:06:52,160
And here they're saying that this matrix

208
00:06:52,160 --> 00:06:54,919
C is shared across all the words. So,

209
00:06:54,919 --> 00:06:56,320
we're always indexing into the same

210
00:06:56,320 --> 00:06:59,480
matrix C over and over.

211
00:06:59,480 --> 00:07:02,040
for each one of these words.

212
00:07:02,040 --> 00:07:03,520
Next up is the hidden layer of this

213
00:07:03,520 --> 00:07:04,720
neural network.

214
00:07:04,720 --> 00:07:06,919
The size of this hidden neural layer of

215
00:07:06,919 --> 00:07:09,360
this neural net is a hyper parameter.

216
00:07:09,360 --> 00:07:10,760
So, we use the word hyper parameter when

217
00:07:10,760 --> 00:07:12,200
it's kind of like a design choice up to

218
00:07:12,200 --> 00:07:13,919
the designer of the neural net. And this

219
00:07:13,919 --> 00:07:15,960
can be as large as you'd like or as

220
00:07:15,960 --> 00:07:17,560
small as you'd like. So, for example,

221
00:07:17,560 --> 00:07:19,360
the size could be 100.

222
00:07:19,360 --> 00:07:20,600
And we are going to go over multiple

223
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:22,840
choices of the size of this hidden

224
00:07:22,840 --> 00:07:24,440
layer, and we're going to evaluate how

225
00:07:24,440 --> 00:07:26,200
well they work.

226
00:07:26,200 --> 00:07:28,280
So, say there were 100 neurons here,

227
00:07:28,280 --> 00:07:29,840
all of them would be fully connected to

228
00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:33,080
the 90 words or 90 um

229
00:07:33,080 --> 00:07:35,960
numbers that make up these three words.

230
00:07:35,960 --> 00:07:38,040
So, this is a fully connected layer.

231
00:07:38,040 --> 00:07:40,240
Then there's a tanh non-linearity.

232
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:42,160
And then there's this output layer. And

233
00:07:42,160 --> 00:07:44,520
because there are 17,000 possible words

234
00:07:44,520 --> 00:07:46,680
that could come next, this layer has

235
00:07:46,680 --> 00:07:49,440
17,000 neurons, and all of them are

236
00:07:49,440 --> 00:07:52,280
fully connected to all of these neurons

237
00:07:52,280 --> 00:07:54,640
in the hidden layer.

238
00:07:54,640 --> 00:07:56,160
So there's a lot of parameters here

239
00:07:56,160 --> 00:07:58,720
because there's a lot of words. So most

240
00:07:58,720 --> 00:07:59,880
computation is here. This is the

241
00:07:59,880 --> 00:08:01,600
expensive layer.

242
00:08:01,600 --> 00:08:04,840
Now there are 17,000 logits here. So on

243
00:08:04,840 --> 00:08:06,960
top of there, we have the softmax layer,

244
00:08:06,960 --> 00:08:08,520
which we've seen in our previous video

245
00:08:08,520 --> 00:08:10,800
as well. So every one of these logits is

246
00:08:10,800 --> 00:08:12,760
exponentiated, and then everything is

247
00:08:12,760 --> 00:08:15,040
normalized to sum to one so that we have

248
00:08:15,040 --> 00:08:17,280
a nice probability distribution for the

249
00:08:17,280 --> 00:08:19,840
next word in the sequence.

250
00:08:19,840 --> 00:08:21,480
Now of course during training, we

251
00:08:21,480 --> 00:08:23,000
actually have the label. We have the

252
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:24,360
identity of the next word in the

253
00:08:24,360 --> 00:08:25,600
sequence.

254
00:08:25,600 --> 00:08:29,919
That word or its index is used to pluck

255
00:08:29,919 --> 00:08:32,760
out the probability of that word,

256
00:08:32,760 --> 00:08:34,880
and then we are maximizing the

257
00:08:34,880 --> 00:08:37,960
probability of that word with respect to

258
00:08:37,960 --> 00:08:39,880
the parameters of this neural net.

259
00:08:39,880 --> 00:08:41,400
So the parameters are the weights and

260
00:08:41,400 --> 00:08:44,640
biases of this output layer, the weights

261
00:08:44,640 --> 00:08:47,320
and biases of this hidden layer, and the

262
00:08:47,320 --> 00:08:49,600
embedding lookup table C. And all of

263
00:08:49,600 --> 00:08:52,320
that is optimized using backpropagation.

264
00:08:52,320 --> 00:08:55,560
And these dashed arrows, ignore those.

265
00:08:55,560 --> 00:08:57,320
That represents a variation of a neural

266
00:08:57,320 --> 00:08:58,760
net that we are not going to explore in

267
00:08:58,760 --> 00:08:59,920
this video.

268
00:08:59,920 --> 00:09:01,320
So that's the setup, and now let's

269
00:09:01,320 --> 00:09:02,480
implement it.

270
00:09:02,480 --> 00:09:04,280
Okay, so I started a brand new notebook

271
00:09:04,280 --> 00:09:05,600
for this lecture.

272
00:09:05,600 --> 00:09:07,760
We are importing PyTorch, and we are

273
00:09:07,760 --> 00:09:09,480
importing Matplotlib so we can create

274
00:09:09,480 --> 00:09:10,800
figures.

275
00:09:10,800 --> 00:09:13,040
Then I am reading all the names into a

